Restore SGUG's IRIX GOT-local classification against binutils 2.47

The guest smoke with vanilla 2.47 dies at startup (SIGSEGV for a plain
o32 hello), and onre's partial port of SGUG-RSE's binutils2_23.sgifixes
patch (binutils-gdb branch binutils-2_44-irix, 4b55be5884a3) still dies
with SIGBUS. Onre re-derived only the first semantic hunk:
mips_use_local_got_p must put only forced-local symbols in the local GOT,
so that locally-binding but preemptible symbols stay in the global GOT
where IRIX rld can fill them. This patch re-derives the second hunk as
well: SGUG's check_forced parameter on mips_elf_local_relocation_p and
the relocation-time local_p test in mips_elf_calculate_relocation, where
2.47's unconditional mips_use_local_got_p also claims dynindx == -1 and
executable static-relocation symbols.

Provenance: SGUG-RSE packages/binutils/binutils2_23.sgifixes.patch
(against binutils 2.23.2: the mips_elf_resolve_final_got_entries hunk and
the mips_elf_local_relocation_p/local_p hunks); onre/binutils-gdb
4b55be5884a302da12b9a96b92134e3aaabf609a for the mips_use_local_got_p
half. Re-derived against binutils 2.47 (sourceware binutils-gdb).

Effect on the o32 hello signature: MIPS_LOCAL_GOTNO 13 -> 9 and
MIPS_GOTSYM 0xd -> 0x9, and on n32 16 -> 12, matching the known-good
SGUG 2.23.2 and 2.20.1 links. The check_forced half has no effect on
that signature; it is carried as SGUG's relocation-time counterpart and
still needs guest exercise of the wider code shapes it served. Guest
validation pending; see docs/binutils.md.


